    {
     "snippet": "🔍 Why is it $O(\\log n)$?",
     "snippet_latex": [
      "O(\\log n)"
     ],
     "type": "heading"
    },
    {
     "snippet": "The binary search algorithm uses a divide-and-conquer approach. Instead of checking elements one by one (like a linear search), it requires the array to be sorted and repeatedly splits the search area in half:",
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "list": [
      {
       "snippet": "You start with n elements."
      },
      {
       "snippet": "After 1 step, you eliminate half the data, leaving $\\frac{n}{2}$ elements.",
       "snippet_latex": [
        "\\frac{n}{2}"
       ]
      },
      {
       "snippet": "After 2 steps, you have $\\frac{n}{4}$ (or $\\frac{n}{2^{2}}$) elements.",
       "snippet_latex": [
        "\\frac{n}{4}",
        "\\frac{n}{2^{2}}"
       ]
      },
      {
       "snippet": "After k steps, you have $\\frac{n}{2^{k}}$ elements.",
       "snippet_latex": [
        "\\frac{n}{2^{k}}"
       ]
      }
     ],
     "type": "list"
    },
    {
     "snippet": "The search terminates in the worst-case scenario when the search space is reduced to just 1 element ( $\\frac{n}{2^k} = 1$).",
     "snippet_latex": [
      "\\frac{n}{2^k} = 1"
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "snippet": "If you solve for k (the number of steps):\n$n=2^{k}$\n$\\log _{2}(n)=k$",
     "snippet_latex": [
      "n=2^{k}",
      "\\log _{2}(n)=k"
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "snippet": "Because big-O notation drops constant bases, this simplifies directly to $O(\\log n)$.",
     "snippet_latex": [
      "O(\\log n)"
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "snippet": "💡 Real-World Scale",
     "type": "heading"
    },
    {
     "snippet": "To understand how fast logarithmic time is, look at how many comparisons it takes to find an item as the dataset grows:",
